{
  "requirement": "Lists the selectable items from a single parse, including the root for the whole file.\n`diagnostic` holds the first syntax error reported during recovery, or `None` for a clean parse.\n`Outline::new` initializes it to `None`.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct Outline {\n    pub items: Vec<OutlineItem>,\n    pub diagnostic: Option<OutlineError>,\n}\n\nimpl Outline {\n    pub fn new(items: Vec<OutlineItem>) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for Outline;\nimpl Debug for Outline;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I found no test-adequacy gap. I did not request implementation source, because the supplied tests answer the questions I had.\n\nObligations and the tests that cover them:\n\n- **`diagnostic` holds the first syntax error on a recovered parse.**\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/tests.rs#fn[reports_recovered_syntax_errors_with_lines]` checks Rust: `diagnostic` is `Some` and its line is 2.\n  - The same test checks Python: `diagnostic.is_some()`.\n  - The same test checks TOML with errors on lines 3 and 4. It asserts the reported error is on line 3 and that its text starts with \"line 3: \". This is what separates \"first\" from \"last\".\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[recovered_outlines_keep_the_diagnostic_and_intact_items]` checks that a recovered Rust outline has line 3 and a \"syntax error\" message. It also checks that the intact items are kept and that `languages::Rust.outline(source)` returns the same diagnostic.\n- **`diagnostic` is `None` for a clean parse.**\n  - `outline_content.rs#fn[rust_items_attach_documentation_and_preserve_structural_hashes]` asserts `diagnostic.is_none()` on a clean Rust parse.\n  - The test `outline_content.rs#fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` covers `Outline::new`, as the next bullet describes.\n- **`Outline::new` initializes `diagnostic` to `None`.** `outline_content.rs#fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` asserts `Outline::new(vec![item]).diagnostic.is_none()`.\n- **`items` lists the selectable items, including the root.**\n  - `outline_content.rs#fn[unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors]` checks the root span, the empty-source outline with a single root item, and the line items.\n  - The Rust items test checks the full selector and kind lists, with the root `\"\"` first.\n  - `outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_languages_claim_extensions_and_supply_the_same_public_outline]` checks the public outline for every bundled language.\n  - `outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments` checks that `Outline::new` and `clone` keep the items.\n- **`Clone` and `Debug`.** `outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments` calls `outline.clone()`, checks the cloned items, and asserts that `format!(\"{outline:?}\")` is non-empty.\n\nThe `#[non_exhaustive]` attribute and the field and signature shapes are public-API declarations. `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` checks the export inventory and that the `habeas` consumer compiles against the SDK. No test would catch a removed `#[non_exhaustive]`, but I treat that as a declaration-level constraint and not a behavioral obligation that needs its own test.",
  "references": "219ac48156eec23906553b8f75acd4bdebe7deafb02150ba8a0d4ef086810c02",
